            <ArticleTitle>A comparative evaluation of intrapleural and thoracic epidural analgesia for postoperative pain relief after minimally invasive direct coronary artery bypass surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>162-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare the efficacy of thoracic epidural analgesia (TEA) and intrapleural analgesia (IPA) after minimally invasive direct coronary artery bypass (MIDCAB) surgery with regard to quality of analgesia and complications.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A prospective, randomized study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">A specialty research hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Fifty consenting adults scheduled for MIDCAB surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">All patients underwent elective MIDCAB surgery. Patients in the TEA group (n=25) had an epidural catheter inserted in the fourth to fifth thoracic interspace and those in the IPA group (n=25) had an intrapleural catheter inserted in the sixth to seventh intercostal space intraoperatively under vision.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Parameters evaluated after administration of bupivacaine (8 mL of 0.25% in the TEA group and 20 mL of 0.25% in the IPA group) on first demand included visual analog scale (VAS) pain scores, cardiovascular and respiratory (clinical, blood gases) function, wakefulness, supplemental analgesic requirement, and complications. Measurements were made at 2-hour intervals for the next 12 hours. VAS scores were significantly lower at 2, 6, 8, and 12 hours in the IPA group (TEA = 3.5, 4.5, 4.9, 4.6; IPA = 2.2, 3.6, 3.5, 3.7). There were no significant differences in hemodynamic or respiratory parameters or postoperative requirement for supplemental analgesia. In the TEA group, three patients had catheter migration and four had severe backache.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">IPA is a safe and effective technique for postoperative analgesia after MIDCAB surgery and has a low complication rate compared with TEA. Careful positioning, chest tube clamping, and anchoring of the catheter are mandatory for IPA to be effective.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
